7.2.m:
Chapter 11
Īśvara said:
Now I shall declare, O ruler of gods, in brief the duties of the varṇas
for qualified devotees,
learned and foremost among the twice-born:
bathing three times a day, performing fire-rites, and regular worship of the liṅga in proper order;

purity, tuft of hair, sacred thread, turban, and upper garment;
refraining from what is forbidden, and wearing ash and rudrākṣa;
worship on festival days, O goddess, especially on the fourteenth day;
and drinking the brahmakūrca month after month according to rule;

likewise [avoiding] stale food, especially barley porridge;
avoiding intoxicating drink, the smell of liquor, and [unlawful] food offerings.
Common to all varṇas, and especially for brāhmaṇas,
are patience, tranquility, contentment, truthfulness, and non-stealing;

Then one should draw [lines] on the fire-pit and so forth in the manner stated in one's own sūtra;
sprinkling it, one should prepare the seat of fire with darbha grass or with flowers.
And one should prepare all substances for worship and for the oblation;
washing those items that need washing, one should purify [them] by sprinkling with the sprinkling-water.

by Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and especially by Rudra;
by the world-guardians headed by Indra, and by the nine planets headed by the Sun;
by great sages who know Brahman, headed by Viśvāmitra and Vasiṣṭha;
by Śveta, Agastya, Dadhīca, and others, and by ourselves who have taken refuge in Śiva;

Only when there is a listener will there be a speaker of slander against yogins;
therefore the listener is even more sinful and deserving of punishment, in the view of the very great.
Those, on the other hand, who constantly revere Śiva-yogins with devotion—
